window.docContents[130].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Application-of-Double-Centralizer-Theorem-to-V_________n___/#Chapter5___Discussion_before_Theorem5___18___2":{"contents":"We will now apply Theorem 5.18.1 to the following situation: E = V^{\\otimes n}, where V is a finite dimensional vector space over a field k of characteristic zero and A is the image of k[S_n] in \\operatorname{End} E. Let us now characterize the algebra B. Let \\mathfrak{gl}(V) be \\operatorname{End} V regarded as a Lie algebra with operation ab - ba.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$\tApplication of Double Centralizer Theorem to V^{⊗n}","header":"Application of Double Centralizer Theorem to V^{⊗n}","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Application-of-Double-Centralizer-Theorem-to-V_________n___/#Chapter5___Discussion_before_Theorem5___18___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/#chapter-05___section-5-9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.9. The Frobenius formula for the character of an induced representation","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/#chapter-05___section-5-9"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Morita-equivalence-classes-and-dimensions-of-algebras/#Chapter9___Discussion_after_Definition9___7___1":{"contents":"Thus, we obtain that Morita equivalence classes of finite dimensional algebras are the collections of the form \\{B_{\\mathbf{n}}(\\mathcal{C}), \\mathbf{n} \\in \\mathbb{N}^m\\}.\n\nNote that the dimension of B_{\\mathbf{n}} is \\sum c_{ij} n_i n_j, where (c_{ij}) is the Cartan matrix of \\mathcal{C}. In particular, the algebra B = B_{\\mathbf{n}} with the smallest possible dimension, \\sum c_{ij}, corresponds to the case when all n_i = 1, i.e., to the projective generator P = \\bigoplus_i P_i. It is easy to see that this algebra is the only one of the B_{\\mathbf{n}} for which B_{\\mathbf{n}}/\\operatorname{Rad}(B_{\\mathbf{n}}) = \\bigoplus_i \\operatorname{Mat}_{n_i}(k) is commutative.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.7. Morita equivalence\tMorita equivalence classes and dimensions of algebras","header":"Morita equivalence classes and dimensions of algebras","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Morita-equivalence-classes-and-dimensions-of-algebras/#Chapter9___Discussion_after_Definition9___7___1"}});
